WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.053
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:00:01.053 --> 00:00:02.770
we're gonna talk about
a few of the pitfalls

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.440
that someone might encounter

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.230
while they're trying to solve

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.930
a quadratic equation like this.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.650
Why is it a quadratic equation?

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:10.520
Well, it's a quadratic

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.530
because it has this second
degree term right over here

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.700
and it's an equation because
I have something on both sides

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:18.320
of an equal sign.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.220
So one strategy that people might try is,

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.710
well, I have something squared,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.360
why don't I just try to take
the square root of both sides?

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.050
And if you did that, you would get

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:33.020
the square root of x squared
plus four x plus three

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.840
is equal to the square
root of negative one.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.760
And you immediately see a few problems.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.460
Even if this wasn't a negative one here,

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:40.700
that's the most obvious problem.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.670
But even if this was
a positive value here,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:43.960
how do you simplify

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.588
or how do you somehow
isolate the x over here?

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:49.870
You've pretty quickly hit a dead end.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.060
So just willy nilly,

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.380
taking the square root of
both sides of a quadratic

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.960
is not going to be too helpful.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.590
So I'll put a big X over there.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.570
Another strategy that sometimes
people will try to go for

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.760
is to isolate the x squared first.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:05.930
So you could imagine,

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:06.790
let me just rewrite it.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.840
X squared plus four x plus
three is equal to negative one.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.880
They might say, let's
isolate that x squared

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.620
by subtracting four x from both sides

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.720
and subtracting three from both sides.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.450
And then what happens?

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.630
On the left hand side,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.000
you do indeed isolate the x squared,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.310
and on the right hand side,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.240
you get negative four x minus four.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.940
And now, someone might say,

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.230
if I take the square root of both sides,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.860
I could get,

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.040
I'll just write that down.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.300
Square root of x squared is equal to,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.440
and you could try to take
the plus of minus of one side

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.040
to make sure you're
hitting the negative roots.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.590
Negative four x minus four.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.110
And you could get something like this,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.500
you would get x is equal to

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.570
plus or minus the square root
of negative four x minus four,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.580
but this still doesn't help you.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:01:59.900
You still don't know what x is,

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:00.970
and it's really not clear

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.600
what to do with this algebraically.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.260
So this is yet another dead end.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:07.420
Now, there's some cases

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.920
in which this strategy would have worked.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.010
In fact, it would have worked

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.750
if you did not have
this first degree term.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.680
If you did not have this
x term, so to speak.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.130
Then this strategy would have worked

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.880
assuming that there are some solutions.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.940
But if you have an x term like this

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.760
and it doesn't cancel out somehow,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.070
you know, if there was another
four x on the other side,

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.850
then you could subtract
four x from both sides,

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:28.870
and they would disappear.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:30.930
But if can't make these things disappear,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.930
this strategy that I've just outlined

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.150
is not going to be a productive one.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.150
Now another strategy that
you'll sometimes see people use,

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.460
especially when they
see something like this,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.360
let me rewrite it.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.560
X squared plus four x plus
three is equal to negative one.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.780
They immediately go into factoring mode.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.770
They say, hey, wait, I think I
might be able to factor this.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.040
I can think of two numbers
that add up to four

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.700
and whose product is three.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.800
Maybe three and one.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.070
And then they immediately factor
this left hand expression,

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.910
say that's going to be x plus three

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.070
times x plus one,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.120
and then that's going to
be equal to negative one.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.880
And then they either are
about to make a mistake,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.880
this is actually algebraically valid.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.270
But they either make a mistake

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.250
or they realize that
they're at a dead end.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.310
Because just saying that
something times something

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.550
is equal to negative one

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.700
doesn't help you a ton.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.700
Because it's not clear
yet, how you'd solve for x.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.810
Another thing, try to do is,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:25.643
is they'll immediately say,

00:03:25.643 --> 00:03:28.240
okay, therefore x is
equal to negative three

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.550
or x is equal to negative one

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.870
because negative three will
make this first term zero

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:33.980
and negative one,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.350
or negative one would
make the second term zero.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.750
But remember, this would only be true

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.250
if you're multiplying two things

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.680
and you got zero as their product,

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.740
then the solutions would be anything

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.140
that made either one of
those terms equal to zero.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.470
But that's not what
we're dealing with here.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.860
Here we're taking the product
is equal to negative one.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:52.360
So in order to factor like this

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.620
and make headway in most cases,

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.160
you're going to wanna have a zero

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.110
on the right hand side over here.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.580
And that's also true

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.520
if you're trying to apply
the quadratic formula.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.830
A lot of folks would say,

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.350
okay, I see a quadratic
equation right over here.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.970
Let me just apply the quadratic formula.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.430
They say, if I have something of the form

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.740
ax squared plus bx plus
c is equal to zero.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.680
The quadratic formula would say

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.240
that the roots are gonna be

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.100
x is equal to negative b

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:23.470
plus or minus the square root
of b squared minus four ac,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.770
all of that over two a.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:26.603
And so they'll immediately say,

00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:29.610
all right, I can recognize a here,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:30.620
as just being a one,

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.760
there's a one coefficient
implicitly there,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.970
b is four, c is three.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.640
And they'll say, okay, x
is equal to negative four

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.410
plus or minus the square
root of b squared,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:45.410
which is 16, minus four
times one, times three,

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:48.120
all of that over two times one.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.650
But there's a problem.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:50.970
The quadratic formula applies

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.060
when the left hand side is equal to zero.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.410
That's not what we have over here.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:58.380
So we're falling into that same pitfall.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.220
So everything I just did,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:02.620
none of this is a good idea.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.600
So the way to approach this,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.590
if you want zero over here,

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.230
you wanna add one on the right hand side,

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.560
and if you wanna maintain the equality,

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.050
you have to add a one
on the left hand side.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.740
And so you're going to get

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:16.740
x squared plus four x plus
four is equal to zero.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.330
And now you could use
the quadratic formula

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.230
or you could factor.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.260
You might recognize two
plus two is equal to four.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.200
Two times two is equal to four.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.910
So you could say x plus two

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:30.120
times x plus two is equal to zero.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.470
And so in this case, you say,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.860
all right, x could be
equal to negative two

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:35.710
or x could be equal to
negative two. (chuckles)

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.630
So this one only has one solution,

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.950
x is equal to negative two.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.290
But the key is to recognize

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.730
that you need the zero on
the right hand side there,

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.610
if you wanna use a quadratic formula

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.943
or if you wanna use factoring
and the zero product property.

